00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.560
OK.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.330
Yeah, a bit more.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:22.440
OK, cool.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.200
But there's one problem.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.320
We showed you that
you changed something,

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.380
and it's very quick to rebuild.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:32.700
But in real life,
building a Docker image

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.710
doesn't take 2 seconds, right?

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:37.680
Who has Docker builds that
deck more than 10 seconds

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:38.850
or 30 seconds, or?

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.020
OK.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.190
It can be very, very long.

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.530
I took a Java example here
to show you the problem

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:44.730
if you don't know it.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.400
So who's a Java developer here?

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.020
OK, same problem applies to
Python or Node, or whatever.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:55.070
It's always faster to build
your applications locally

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.340
on your machine than
with Docker containers.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.100
So here is a Java application.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.970
So usually, to build it,
you would run such command.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.998
Because it was built
already on my machine,

00:08:04.998 --> 00:08:06.540
it takes only a few
seconds to build.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.050
I can build it two times--
it takes 2 seconds, OK?

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:13.867
Now, I've got a Docker file.

00:08:13.867 --> 00:08:15.450
I want to containerize
my application.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.000
So I've got a Docker file which
is already quite complicated

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:18.812
to write.

00:08:18.812 --> 00:08:20.520
There's quite a few
tricks in that Docker

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.850
file to make it nice with you.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:31.323
And I'm going to
build my application.

00:08:31.323 --> 00:08:33.740
It's going to be very quick,
because I've built it already

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:34.669
on my machine.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:37.605
But now, I'm going to change
something in the code.

00:08:37.605 --> 00:08:38.855
I'm going to change a comment.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:42.500
Oops, where am I?

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.403
I'm going to change just
the comment here, OK?

00:08:48.403 --> 00:08:49.820
How long do you
think it will take

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.860
to rebuild the Docker image?

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:54.500
10 seconds?

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.730
One minute?

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:58.080
Two minutes?

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.540
Four minutes?

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.620
OK.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:03.080
The thing is, is that
it's going to redownload

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:07.400
all the dependencies from my
projects-- all the plugins.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.170
So it's going to take maybe four
minutes on a slow connection.

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.380
And it's going to be more than
that on a very slow connection.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.210
So this is the problem
with Docker builds.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.320
It has a smart
caching mechanism,

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.640
but it will never be as quick as
what you have on your machine.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.310
Because here, I change
something in the pom.yml.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:28.130
It has no impact on
what's on my artifacts,

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:31.800
on my build artifact, but it
has an impact on Docker caching.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.090
So it has to redownload all the
plugins, which is really not

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:35.900
nice.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:39.620
Just that far a "Hello
World!" application.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:42.760
So we're going to show you a
second open-source tool called

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:43.780
Jib.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:46.870
Jib stands for
Java Image Builder.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:47.900
It's open-source.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.140
You can also find it
on GitHub, same org--

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:51.550
Google Container Tools.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.460
It's a Maven and
Gradle plugin that you

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:59.110
can use to build Docker images
without even installing Docker

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:03.185
without writing a Docker file,
without learning the Docker

00:10:03.185 --> 00:10:04.370
file syntax.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.000
And it gives you fast,
incremental builds.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.980
So I think the Java
developers in the room

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.300
will really like it.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:15.720
All you have to do
is add a new plugin

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.970
to your Java configuration--

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:19.590
just that.

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.720
You select this Jib plugin.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.000
It's the same application.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.490
And now I'm going to run a
slightly different command.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:29.340
I'm going to do a maven
compile jib:build,

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.602
and give it the name of the
image that it should produce.

00:10:32.602 --> 00:10:35.610
And with that command, it
will build my application.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.130
And when it's done, it's going
to produce a Docker image

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:41.040
and push it in 4 seconds.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:48.790
I can change the comment in the
YAML if you want and rebuild.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.255
It should also be
about 4 seconds.

00:10:51.255 --> 00:10:53.380
Because everything was
already built on my machine,

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.330
it's using the cache
that's on my machine.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.620
It's not using the Docker cache.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.580
So maybe you think that's
not very important.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.780
But when you're using skaffold
dev, and you change something,

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.510
and you focus on your code,
you don't want the rebuild

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:06.890
to take a few minutes.

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:09.510
You want it to be as
quick as possible.

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:10.920
Jib is one solution.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.760
There are other solutions that
we won't talk about, but just

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.772
know that there
are people who try

00:11:15.772 --> 00:11:17.480
to make it very quick
for you so that you

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:18.510
can focus on your code.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:28.710
One last thing I wanted to say
is that Skaffold and Jib is not

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.103
just for "Hello World!"

00:11:30.103 --> 00:11:31.770
It works very well
with a "Hello World!"

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.800
simple, but here we have
a much bigger project.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.650
It's a multi-microservices
application

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:39.930
based on six microservices.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.900
Each of them has a Docker
image, and each of them

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.720
is in a separate directory.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.110
And we have more YAML
manifests-- yeah, YAML--

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:52.320
what we want to deploy.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.660
And because I've got
this Skaffold YAML,

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.410
I can run skaffold dev, and it's
going to rebuild all my images.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:11:59.610
It's going to tag them.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.860
It's going to put all the
tags in the YAML files,

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:03.780
deploy everything,
show me the logs

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.610
for all the containers at once.

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:07.110
See?

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:10.120
It's deployed, and
I can see the logs.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:11.452
That's nice, right?

00:12:11.452 --> 00:12:12.910
So it's not just
for "Hello World!"

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:16.450
And if I change something,
because I'm dev mode--

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:20.080
like, for example, I will
change some Java application,

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:21.340
some Java code.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.230
I'm going to change
the title here.

00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:27.115
It's the Google Cloud
Quiz for Next '19.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:31.690
Save it.

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.130
And boom, Skaffold
will detect that,

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:38.830
will run jib build, and redeploy
this application for me.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.140
So it deployed
only what's needed.

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.160
And boom, my
application is there.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.100
I have a very quick feedback.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:49.720
And if I change something,
like a static web page,

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.640
it can be even faster.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:53.110
I'm going to add
some exclamation

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.662
marks, because I like them.

00:12:54.662 --> 00:12:55.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.330
Boom, it's already deployed.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:02.260
Let me do that for you again,
because it was too quick.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:02.760
OK.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.470
Save, boom.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:08.260
Skaffold synced the
file from my machine

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.140
to the running pod
on a remote cluster.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:12.980
I don't even have to
rebuild the images.

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:16.490
So it's really as quick as
if I was working locally.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:20.530
So you really get the 10x
velocity with Skaffold.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:28.570
So this is a schema just to
explain what Skaffold did.

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:30.810
So you add all these
components-- the UI, the title,

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:33.070
the game, the
database, et cetera--

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.780
all of them in
different folders.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.870
Skaffold will watch the sources
for all those components.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:40.600
And for some components,
it will sync the files

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:42.070
to the remote cluster.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:45.202
For some components, it will
run jib build to make sure

00:13:45.202 --> 00:13:47.410
that the Java application
is rebuilt on your machine.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:48.868
And for the rest,
it will do Docker

00:13:48.868 --> 00:13:51.370
build, which will be a
little bit slower, but still

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:52.840
very, very nice.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.180
And all the ports for
all the containers

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:58.300
are ports forwarded
locally on my machine.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:00.615
So that if I connect
to remote cluster--

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.570
I'm going to connect
on GKE, which is

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:09.137
a cluster, always more nodes.

00:14:09.137 --> 00:14:09.637
Nope.

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:13.610
OK.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:16.980
I can run the same
skaffold dev command,

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.780
and this time it needs to
push the images remotely,

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:22.940
but most of them
were already pushed,

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:24.410
and Skaffold knows that.

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:26.990
It's got a very smart,
aggressive caching mechanism

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:30.133
that Priya developed.

00:14:30.133 --> 00:14:31.550
It's the reason
why it's so quick.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.200
But some of the images are
changed in front of you,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:35.090
so they need to be
rebuilt and pushed.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.520
And when it's done, I
will be able to connect

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.530
to my remote
cluster the same way

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:42.320
as I was connecting
on the local cluster.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:45.400
It's almost there.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:46.410
Yeah.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:47.860
So I'm connected on local host.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.010
But in fact, I'm connected
to my remote cluster

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:51.710
thanks to port forwarding.

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:54.060
And I've got the
exact same experience.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:15:01.080
If I change something here--
more exclamation marks--

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.900
it's going to be
synced automatically.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:04.410
Was it?

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:05.190
Yeah.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:06.600
I think it was.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.100
No, maybe not.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:08.770
There's a bug.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:09.830
OK, it happens.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.516
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:16.390
That's Skaffold.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.530
PRIYA WADHWA: That's Skaffold.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:22.440
So David just talked about
using Jib to make your builds

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:23.670
a little bit faster.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:27.060
Skaffold also
supports using Bazel.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.920
So you can build
locally with Docker,

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.290
you can build on the
cloud with Google Cloud,

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.810
or you can build your images
on a Kubernetes cluster

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.100
with Kaniko.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.740
We also support testing with
container structure tests

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.840
and deploying your application
with kubectl, Kustomize,

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:41.710
or Helm.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.000
So if you use any of these
tools already in your Kubernetes

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.310
application
development workflow,

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:48.750
you can just keep using them.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.150
As long as you put that stuff
into your Skaffold YAML,

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:53.940
Skaffold will be smart enough
to know to deploy or build

00:15:53.940 --> 00:15:56.088
with the tools
you're already using.

00:15:56.088 --> 00:15:56.880
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.950
This is very important
for the Skaffold team

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:02.030
that you keep the tools
you like, like Help,

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.320
or Kustomize, a kubectl.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.450
And they are
integrated in Skaffold

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:08.420
so that you can just
focus on the code.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:11.850
And it will work with
any Kubernetes cluster--

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:16.080
GKE, any cloud provider,
on-prem, local, local desktop,

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:16.987
Minikube.

00:16:16.987 --> 00:16:17.820
It's very important.

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:19.892
If you have a hybrid
setup or on-prem setup,

00:16:19.892 --> 00:16:20.850
it works very well too.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.477
So it gives us very
tight feedback loop.

00:16:26.477 --> 00:16:27.810
And you should try it on Monday.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:29.105
Like, Monday, OK?

00:16:29.105 --> 00:16:31.878
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:31.878 --> 00:16:32.670
PRIYA WADHWA: Cool.

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:35.700
So David show us how to
use Skaffold as a CLI tool.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.010
But can it be even easier?

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.010
And, yes, it can.

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.040
So one of the teams
at Google just

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.140
released Cloud Code,
which is an IDE

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:50.850
extension that runs Skaffold
for you directly from your IDE.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.220
So it basically does
everything we just showed.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:56.250
Skaffold runs skaffold
dev, but from the comfort

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:59.220
of IntelliJ or VS Code, if
you use either of those.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.346
DAVID GAGEOT: Who's
using Vs code here?

00:17:01.346 --> 00:17:02.980
IntelliJ?

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:03.480
OK.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:04.420
So all the room.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:04.920
Almost.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.270
PRIYA WADHWA: Awesome.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.290
Cool.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:11.500
So let's do a quick demo.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:16.140
So I have this really
retro game of Tic-Tac-Toe.

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.288
And it's not very
colorful, so let's make

00:17:19.288 --> 00:17:20.330
it a little bit prettier.

00:17:20.330 --> 00:17:21.869
DAVID GAGEOT: You deployed
this on GKE, right?

00:17:21.869 --> 00:17:22.369
Yeah.

00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:23.940
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah,
deployed on GKE.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:26.730
So it's pretty simple,
just two microservices.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.460
I have a Tic-Tac-Toe front
end written in JavaScript

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:33.570
and a colors microservice
written in Go.

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:38.380
And basically, this file
tells the application

00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:41.640
what colors I want my X's and
O's to be for Tic-Tac-Toe.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.290
So if I want to
change the colors

00:17:43.290 --> 00:17:45.565
and make them a little
more interesting--

00:17:45.565 --> 00:17:46.690
what's your favorite color.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:47.130
David?

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:48.110
DAVID GAGEOT: I
don't know, blue.

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:49.200
PRIYA WADHWA: I
already did that one.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:49.340
OK.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:50.290
Green or purple.

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:51.020
DAVID GAGEOT: OK.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:53.595
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:57.420
PRIYA WADHWA: So you saw I just
made some changes to my IDE,

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:58.795
and with Cloud
code, I don't even

00:17:58.795 --> 00:18:02.590
have to leave the IDE to deploy
these changes to my cluster.

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:05.490
So you can see that with
Cloud Code I can deploy,

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:07.920
I can create a new GKE cluster.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:09.690
I can run a continuous
deploy, which

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:12.570
is basically just the skaffold
dev loop that David was just

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:13.500
showing.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.410
Every time I save a file in
my ID it'll rebuild my image

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:18.870
and push it to my cluster again.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:21.690
So for now, I'm just going
to deploy my application

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:22.900
to my cluster.

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:24.510
And the cool thing
about Cloud Code

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:26.340
is if I have
different profiles, I

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.800
can choose to only deploy a
certain part of my application.

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.610
So I know I only changed
the colors microservice,

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:35.157
so I'm only going to
redeploy that piece.

00:18:35.157 --> 00:18:36.740
DAVID GAGEOT: You
don't have to do it,

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:39.830
but it's nice to have
profiles to tweak your

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.588
build process or debug process.

00:18:41.588 --> 00:18:42.380
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah.

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:44.463
We can take a look at the
Skaffold YAML, actually,

00:18:44.463 --> 00:18:46.130
and what that looks like.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.890
But I have just a
simple Skaffold YAML

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:54.050
here with my two microservices
and path to the Docker files

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:54.977
for those images.

00:18:54.977 --> 00:18:57.560
And then a profile for my colors
microservice, which will only

00:18:57.560 --> 00:19:00.350
build and deploy that bit,
and then one for my front end

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:01.720
as well.

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.710
And if we take a look
at our Cloud Code logs,

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.950
we basically just did a Skaffold
run again, except this time,

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:07.970
directly from our IDE.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:09.470
And we went through
the same process

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.970
that we've already been through,
except Cloud Code is really

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:15.350
nice, and it'll tell me when
my deployment has rolled out

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:17.340
and is successfully deployed.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:20.130
So if we head back
over to our game,

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:21.570
we can see we have some colors.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:22.530
DAVID GAGEOT: Colors.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:23.988
PRIYA WADHWA: So
it was super easy.

00:19:23.988 --> 00:19:26.410
I never had to leave my IDE.

00:19:26.410 --> 00:19:30.010
And it also told me when it was
finished, which is really cool.

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:33.450
So Cloud Code also have
some fun other things.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:35.640
It does Skaffold
YAML validation.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:38.130
So if I wanted to add
an artifact here--

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:42.935
say I have a third microservice
which does something,

00:19:42.935 --> 00:19:44.850
except this one is
built with Bazel.

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:46.770
So I can say start
typing in Bazel,

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:51.600
and Cloud Code will autofill
the rest of the YAML for me.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.250
And I just have to put
in my Bazel target.

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:57.090
And also, we'll do
some validation.

00:19:57.090 --> 00:19:59.490
So if I put in an integer
here, which it doesn't expect,

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:00.660
it'll let me know.

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.510
And if I hover over different
pieces of my Skaffold YAML.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:05.640
it'll tell me what
those pieces mean

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:08.160
and what Skaffold
is expecting, which

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:10.978
is just really nice during
my development process.

00:20:10.978 --> 00:20:11.770
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:14.370
Cloud code gives you this
validation and documentation

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.970
on Skaffold YAML, but any
YAML manifests for Kubernetes.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.100
So your deployments,
[? pods, ?] et cetera,

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:22.663
you have the online
documentation and validation.

00:20:22.663 --> 00:20:23.580
PRIYA WADHWA: Exactly.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:25.410
So it just makes the
Kubernetes development

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:26.270
a little bit easier.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:28.830
Because I always have
to go and look up

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:33.770
what a pod spec is meant to look
like before I write a new one.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:34.480
Cool.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:34.980
Yeah.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.610
So with Cloud Code for VS
Code, continuous deployment,

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.890
you can still run your
skaffold run and skaffold dev,

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:43.670
except this time just with a
button click from your IDE.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:45.810
YAML manifest
validation and more--

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:47.600
so you can download
that today as well.

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:48.807
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah.

00:20:48.807 --> 00:20:51.140
PRIYA WADHWA: And then Cloud
Code for IntelliJ as well--

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:52.520
very similar.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:54.620
But basically, it'll just
run Skaffold for you,

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.960
and you can keep focusing
on writing your code, which

00:20:56.960 --> 00:21:00.212
is the best part.

00:21:00.212 --> 00:21:00.920
DAVID GAGEOT: OK.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:04.890
So we showed how
to develop locally,

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.150
whether it's on the command
line or using your favorite IDE.

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.463
But at some point, your
application will get bigger,

00:21:10.463 --> 00:21:12.380
and you'll want to be
able to be on the cloud.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.880
Because building on your
machine has its limits.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:16.880
Especially if you have
lots of microservices,

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:19.910
you don't want to build all
of them in sequence locally.

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:22.540
So we're going to talk
about one of the managed

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:25.820
services we have on Google
Cloud, which is Cloud Build.

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:30.200
Cloud Build is
fully-manage, CI/CD product

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:33.230
that gives you extremely
fast builds that you can use

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:34.910
to automate your deployments.

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:38.240
So you can build Docker
images or any binaries,

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:41.690
you can run your tests, you
can run your integration tests,

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:45.150
you can deploy at the end
of your bill pipeline.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.870
So you should really take
a look at Cloud Build,

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:49.800
and we're going to
show you how to use it.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:53.120
So usually, to use
Cloud Build, you

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:58.550
end up submitting a Cloud Build
YAML through the command line

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:00.330
or through the UI.

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:02.390
This is a typical command
that you would run--

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:03.970
gcloud build submit,
and you give you

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:05.150
it the Cloud Build YAML.

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:07.730
And this is the content
of the Cloud Build YAML.

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:11.870
This is a list of steps that
are going to run on your code.

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:15.320
So Cloud Build will
clone you repo,

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:19.640
and will run Docker images
steps on your codebase.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:21.580
For example, here,
this first step

00:22:21.580 --> 00:22:25.430
will run NPM install
on your sources.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:27.920
And then NPM run
test to make sure

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:29.600
that the unit tests are run.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:31.490
And then it will
do a Docker build

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:33.130
to make sure to
produce a Docker image,

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.660
and this Docker image will
be pushed by Cloud Build.

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:39.800
So if I run this
command from my sources,

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:44.090
it will send my sources
and run all those commands

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:45.590
so that at the end
I've got a Docker

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.440
image that was not built on my
machine but built on the cloud.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.840
But it's even easier if
you're using Skaffold.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:55.880
I just wanted to
show you a few UIs,

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:58.260
but Priya is going
to show it later on.

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.210
So with Skaffold,
it's even easier.

00:23:00.210 --> 00:23:03.940
Here's my small hexagon
example with all the artifacts,

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:07.250
and I added a small
profile just four lines.

00:23:07.250 --> 00:23:09.170
And with this
profile, you can just

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:11.550
say, OK, if this
profile is activated,

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.030
it will not be locally on
your machine using Docker,

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:17.630
but you will build remotely on
the cloud, all the artifacts

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:18.700
in parallel.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:19.700
Let me show that to you.

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:23.990
I'm going to just
switch back to Docker.

00:23:23.990 --> 00:23:27.260
So I'm going to do a Skaffold
build not using this profile,

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:30.470
just to show you that it's
building locally using Docker.

00:23:30.470 --> 00:23:32.310
And all the artifacts
were already built,

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:35.070
so Skaffold is very
quick to tell you that.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:39.360
And I'm going to now activate
the Cloud Build profile.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.870
So now, this time, Skaffold
should send resources

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.930
to Cloud Build and compile all
the artifacts on the cloud.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:49.560
But because it's smart,
thanks to Priya's code,

00:23:49.560 --> 00:23:51.720
it's going to find that
all those images already

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:53.250
exist remotely.

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.240
Some of them exist
with a different tag,

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:56.940
so it's just going
to retag them.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:24:00.640
It's not even going to send
the source to Cloud Build.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:01.870
That's nice, right?

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:04.820
So you can really
build very quickly.

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:06.520
But I'm going to
change something.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:08.660
I'm going to change--

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:11.860
I don't know-- the UI here--

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:13.750
exclamation marks.

00:24:13.750 --> 00:24:15.637
I told you I wanted
exclamation marks.

00:24:15.637 --> 00:24:16.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:19.360
I'm going to skaffold
build with the profile.

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:22.680
This time, one of
the artifact does not

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.490
exist remotely in my registry.

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:28.530
So Skaffold will take the
sources, put them in a tarball,

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:30.910
sned it to Cloud
Build, trigger a Cloud

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:35.340
build run, which will then run
some commands on top of it.

00:24:35.340 --> 00:24:37.020
Basically, you would
do a Docker build

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:38.517
and a Docker push at the end.

00:24:38.517 --> 00:24:39.600
And it will be very quick.

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.100
And it's even
quicker when you have

00:24:41.100 --> 00:24:43.360
lots of artifacts to be
rebuilt, because it's going

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.370
to build them in parallel.

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:49.290
And when it's done,
triggered will tell me.

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:52.480
So you see, you don't even
have to learn the Cloud Build

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:57.570
CLI, or API, or UI, you can
use the same tool, Skaffold,

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:00.300
on your Cloud Code
extension, and you're

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:01.600
going to build on the cloud.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:02.580
Which is quite nice, right?

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:05.250
PRIYA WADHWA: And you don't have
to write that Cloud Build YAML.

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:06.510
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:07.950
You don't have to learn it.

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:09.168
One less YAML file.

00:25:09.168 --> 00:25:09.960
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.262
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:11.262 --> 00:25:11.970
That's the dream.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:13.512
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah,
that's the Dream.

00:25:13.512 --> 00:25:15.110
Kill them all.

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:18.060
OK, Cloud Build with
Skaffold-- so it builds

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:19.830
all the artifacts in parallel.

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:22.890
It has a very aggressive,
smart-caching mechanisms,

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:27.160
so that any image that was
already built and pushed, even

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.910
was a different tag, you don't
have to go through the whole

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:36.300
build process, and you don't
have to add an additional YAML.

00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:38.702
You should really
try Cloud Build.

00:25:38.702 --> 00:25:40.410
It's very efficient
for that, but there's

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:42.780
also lots of
additional use cases

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:46.390
that you might want to explore.

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:47.128
For example--

00:25:47.128 --> 00:25:47.920
PRIYA WADHWA: Cool.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:50.995
So David just talked about how
we can use Skaffold and Cloud

00:25:50.995 --> 00:25:54.730
Build together to build our
images remotely on the cloud

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:56.500
while doing local development.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.020
But we can also use Cloud
Build and Skaffold together

00:25:59.020 --> 00:26:01.840
to create a continuous
deployment pipeline which

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:05.290
will push your application
to your Kubernetes

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:07.123
cluster for you.

00:26:07.123 --> 00:26:09.040
So let's talk about what
this would look like.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:11.950
Say you're a developer, and
your code lives on GitHub,

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:15.123
and you want to and add a new
feature to your codebase.So

00:26:15.123 --> 00:26:16.540
the first thing
you're going to do

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.960
is develop locally, hopefully
with Skaffold now, and get

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:21.340
your feature up and running.

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:22.990
And then push those
changes to GitHub,

00:26:22.990 --> 00:26:24.670
and as soon as they're
merged to master,

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:27.110
that's where our
pipeline will come in.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:28.765
So Google Cloud Build--

00:26:28.765 --> 00:26:30.640
we're going to use a
Google Cloud Build build

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:31.990
trigger to watch your repo.

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:34.690
And it'll know whenever
you've merged a new change

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:35.650
into your codebase.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:39.580
And once it sees that you have,
it'll pick up your new codebase

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:42.700
and run a skaffold
run on the codebase.

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:45.850
And that'll redeploy
your new feature

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:48.310
to your cluster,
at which point you

00:26:48.310 --> 00:26:53.120
can visualize whatever changes
you made as you normally would.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:55.240
So this is the
pipeline configuration.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:57.137
We have another
Cloud Build YAML.

00:26:57.137 --> 00:26:58.720
But this is basically
what Cloud build

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:02.740
is going to run on
every code change.

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:04.320
So the first step
is pretty simple--

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:06.070
it'll just get credentials
for the cluster

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:08.338
that you want to deploy
your application to.

00:27:08.338 --> 00:27:10.630
And the second step is just
a skaffold run, which we've

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:11.900
done a bunch of times now.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:14.470
So it'll rebuild your images,
push them, and then redeploy

00:27:14.470 --> 00:27:15.820
your application for you.

00:27:15.820 --> 00:27:16.720
DAVID GAGEOT: I just
want to say something

00:27:16.720 --> 00:27:17.830
that's very important.

00:27:17.830 --> 00:27:20.180
We showed you you can use
Skaffold on your machine.

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:23.860
But you can use it on Cloud
Build or CI/CD system.

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:26.710
Because since you've
listed all the artifacts

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:28.810
you want to build,
since you've listed

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:30.850
how you want to
deploy them, you can

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:35.000
do that from Jenkins or
anywhere for that matter.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:37.960
So really, this is what
Priya is showing you.

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:40.150
She's going to run
Skaffold from Cloud Build,

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:41.710
instead of what
we've shown where we

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:43.610
ran Cloud Build from Skaffold.

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:43.740
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:44.200
DAVID GAGEOT: Which is--

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:44.800
OK.

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:46.675
PRIYA WADHWA: You can
do it any way you want,

00:27:46.675 --> 00:27:48.480
in whatever combination.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:50.960
Well, let's do a quick
demo of how this works.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:54.330
So I'm going to go back to my
colors and change them again.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:56.230
Except this time,
let's say we're

00:27:56.230 --> 00:27:58.210
pushing to a production
environment instead

00:27:58.210 --> 00:27:59.360
of a staging environment.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:01.300
So I'm going to want
a version control,

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:05.320
and not just deploy my
changes directly from my IDE.

00:28:05.320 --> 00:28:08.890
So I'm going to commit
these changes to GitHub.

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.620
David, does it look good you?

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:11.320
Code review.

00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:12.770
DAVID GAGEOT: Sorry?

00:28:12.770 --> 00:28:14.230
PRIYA WADHWA: I'm
going say, yes.

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:15.340
I'm just going to
push to master.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:16.820
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah, I like
the color, so you can push.

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:17.740
PRIYA WADHWA: Cool.

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:19.358
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:19.358 --> 00:28:21.400
So now that I've pushed
to master, at this point,

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.310
Google Cloud Build should pick
up the change in my codebase

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:25.870
and start a new build.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:27.910
So we should check the UI.

00:28:27.910 --> 00:28:30.190
And looks like a build
has already started.

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:32.470
And it'll basically
run the two steps

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:35.930
that I already showed in
the pipeline configuration.

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:38.810
So the first step gets
credentials from my cluster.

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:41.680
And the second step
will do a skaffold run.

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:43.180
And I've actually
set up my build

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:46.220
triggers so that they only run
when certain files in GitHub

00:28:46.220 --> 00:28:46.870
are changed.

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.060
So this build trigger runs when
only my colors microservice has

00:28:50.060 --> 00:28:50.560
changed.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:53.290
And it'll only rebuild that
part of the application just

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.050
to speed up my
deploy a little bit.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:28:59.770
DAVID GAGEOT: So you were in
your IDE, you just Git pushed,

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:01.328
and you've got a whole CI/CD--

00:29:01.328 --> 00:29:02.120
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:04.160
DAVID GAGEOT: --chain
that is triggered.

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:06.840
It will build all the artifacts.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:10.000
It's will redeploy everything to
the production cluster, right?

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:10.270
PRIYA WADHWA: Exactly.

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:11.062
DAVID GAGEOT: Cool.

00:29:11.062 --> 00:29:14.470
PRIYA WADHWA: And if we
take a look at the logs,

00:29:14.470 --> 00:29:16.070
we've downloaded
our Skaffold image,

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:17.838
and now we're
rebuilding the image.

00:29:17.838 --> 00:29:20.380
DAVID GAGEOT: In reality, you
don't have to look at the logs,

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:21.290
right?

00:29:21.290 --> 00:29:22.082
PRIYA WADHWA: Yeah.

00:29:22.082 --> 00:29:23.530
You don't actually have to.

00:29:23.530 --> 00:29:24.430
It will just happen.

00:29:28.326 --> 00:29:29.913
And we're pushing.

00:29:29.913 --> 00:29:31.080
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah, pushing.

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.210
PRIYA WADHWA: And we
should be done soon.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:35.163
Usually this takes
around a minute.

00:29:35.163 --> 00:29:36.090
DAVID GAGEOT: Yeah.

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:37.257
PRIYA WADHWA: And it's done.

00:29:37.257 --> 00:29:39.400
So if we head back
to our application,

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:42.370
we should see that the
colors have changed.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:45.020
So that is a really simple
example using Cloud Build

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:47.400
and Skaffold together to
create a continuous deployment

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:49.025
pipeline.

00:29:49.025 --> 00:29:51.400
DAVID GAGEOT: Sometimes it's
so quick that it feels slow.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:53.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:53.740 --> 00:29:54.990
You don't have to do anything.

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:55.823
You just Git pushed.

00:29:55.823 --> 00:29:57.200
It's deployed but--

00:29:57.200 --> 00:29:59.330
PRIYA WADHWA: And then
have a sit-around.

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:01.350
Get a cup of coffee.

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:02.120
DAVID GAGEOT: OK.

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:05.450
So a quick recap
of what we've seen.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:10.310
So we've seen different ways to
develop faster with Kubernetes.

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:12.710
All of them are
connected to Skaffold,

00:30:12.710 --> 00:30:14.840
which is the tool
we are working on

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:16.780
and that's used
underneath by Cloud Code.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:20.040
So Skaffold gives you a
very tight feedback loop.

00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:23.080
Whether you're working
locally, on a local cluster,

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:27.980
or remotely, you can work on
any type of Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:29.060
And we've seen JIb.

00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:32.690
Jib is one of the tools you
can use to build your Docker

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:38.120
images quicker and more easily
because you don't have to learn

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:39.110
the Docker file syntax.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:41.060
You don't have to
install Docker.

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:44.810
It will just use your
standard build tool

00:30:44.810 --> 00:30:46.750
to build Docker images.

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:47.250
Yeah.

00:30:47.250 --> 00:30:48.042
PRIYA WADHWA: Cool.

00:30:48.042 --> 00:30:50.780
We looked at Cloud Code, which
lets you run Skaffold directly

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:52.130
from your IDE.

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:53.838
So you just have to
hit some buttons,

00:30:53.838 --> 00:30:56.130
and I'll do all the work for
you, which is really nice.

00:30:56.130 --> 00:30:59.120
And then we also looked
at Cloud Build, which

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:01.610
can build your images
remotely on the cloud,

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:04.310
and also will help you
set up a full [? CD ?]

00:31:04.310 --> 00:31:07.090
pipeline for your
Kubernetes application.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:07.910
DAVID GAGEOT: OK.

